@* IconButton's Overview, written by hand after Button's: labels and variants, sizes and states,
   actions on a row (previewed in each mode) and a tooltip on focus. Accessibility and See also
   come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>An icon button is a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor> with one icon and no visible text. It suits actions the user knows by their icon: edit, delete, more. When the icon alone isn't clear, use a Button with text.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>An icon button has no visible text, so its label is its name for screen readers and its tooltip. Set <Code>Label</Code> to what the button does; it's required. <Code>Variant</Code>, <Code>Accent</Code> and <Code>Href</Code> work as on Button.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="icon-button-variants" />
<TextBlock><Code>Ghost</Code> with a <Code>Neutral</Code> accent suits rows of tools, where a filled button for each would be loud. Keep a solid one for the action that matters most.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and states</Heading>
<TextBlock>An icon button has the same sizes and states as a Button. <Code>Size</Code>, <Code>Loading</Code> and <Code>Disabled</Code> work as on Button.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="icon-button-sizes-states" />

<Heading Level="2">Toggle buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toggle button turns something on and off, such as a call's microphone. A click toggles it, and screen readers hear whether it's pressed. On interactive pages, set <Code>@@bind-Pressed</Code>. Keep the label the same whether it's pressed or not.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="icon-button-toggle" />
<TextBlock>On static pages, set <Code>Pressed</Code> from the server's state, and post the change with <Code>Submit</Code>, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>. A link can't be pressed. In a <Anchor Href="docs/components/toolbar">Toolbar</Anchor>, a toggle that overflows becomes a checkbox item in the menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Actions on a row</Heading>
<TextBlock>Icon buttons often act on one row of a list, such as deleting a file. For a link, set <Code>Href</Code>. To post which row a button is on, set <Code>Submit</Code>, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>. On <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor>, set <Code>OnClick</Code> to do the same in C#.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="icon-button-row-actions" />
<TextBlock>Put the row in the label too ("Delete budget-2026.xlsx"), so screen readers and the tooltip name which one. <Code>OnClick</Code> runs only on interactive pages. On static pages, the form post is what happens, as with Button.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A floating action button</Heading>
<TextBlock><Code>Floating</Code> fixes the button to the window's bottom corner, raised and large, where it stays as the page scrolls. Use it for the page's main action, such as adding an item, and keep one per page. <Code>FloatPosition</Code> picks the bottom start or end corner or the center. In an <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor> it sits above a sticky footer.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="icon-button-floating" />
<TextBlock>For a floating button with text, set <Code>Floating</Code> on a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A tooltip on focus</Heading>
<TextBlock>The label's tooltip shows on hover. For a hint that also shows on keyboard focus, or for more than the label, such as a shortcut, wrap the button in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/tooltip">Tooltip</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@WithTooltip</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>A loading icon button has a word for screen readers. To set it for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>LoadingLabel</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear before the label while <Code>Loading</Code> is set, as in "Loading Delete"</td><td>"Loading"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <IconButton Icon="Lucide.Pencil" Label="Edit" Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" Accent="Accent.Neutral" />

        @* A link. *@
        <IconButton Icon="Lucide.ExternalLink" Label="Open the docs" Href="https://example.com/docs" />

        @* In a form: posts delete=42 when pressed. *@
        <IconButton Icon="Lucide.Trash" Label="Delete invoice 42" Submit Name="delete" Value="42"
                    Accent="Accent.Danger" Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" />
        """;

    private const string WithTooltip = """
        <Tooltip>
            <Content>Bold <Kbd Keys="Ctrl+B" /></Content>
            <ChildContent><IconButton Icon="Lucide.Bold" Label="Bold" Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" /></ChildContent>
        </Tooltip>
        """;
}
